# The operator trust root must be the same bytes on every machine that checks it out.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. There was no .gitattributes here, and the default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows
# checks text files out with CRLF. Measured on this machine: the repo-local `core.autocrlf=false` hid the
# problem, while the global setting is `true`, so a plain `git clone` produced a 116-byte
# keys/delegation-operator.pub (3 CR) where the committed blob is 113 bytes. The ledger audit compares
# that file against the local ~/.dsh copy and against the key derived from the private PEM, so the CRLF
# checkout made it abort with "pinned public keys disagree" when the keys were byte-identical apart from
# line endings. The scripts now normalise line endings before comparing, and warn when they have to; this
# prevents the conversion in the first place rather than depending on that tolerance.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalises the stored blob to LF too. The committed blobs are already LF, so this changes
# no existing object -- it only stops a checkout from rewriting them.
#
# AND THE SAME REASON APPLIES TO EVERY SOURCE AND SCRIPT FILE, which is why the rule is now general rather
# than limited to the key. MEASURED in a throwaway clone on this host, with global `core.autocrlf=true`:
# tools/currency.ts arrived with 9 carriage returns, plugin/src/verification.ts with 558, README.md with
# 761 -- and scripts/check-file-hygiene.cjs failed that checkout with exit 1. Two consequences, and the
# second is the serious one. A clean clone could not pass `npm run gate` on Windows at all. And a SIGNER
# running there would hash the CRLF bytes on disk and record a digest the anchored tree does not contain,
# producing a note the audit then rejects; the audit itself is tree-backed and survives a CRLF checkout,
# but the signer does not. That is the failure this rule prevents rather than detects.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text, so binary assets are untouched; `eol=lf` forces the CHECKOUT to
# LF rather than to the platform default, which `text=auto` alone would leave as CRLF on Windows.
# `git add --renormalize .` after adding this reported exactly ONE stored blob to rewrite --
# experiments/delegation-ab/.verifier/verify-judge.cjs, committed with CRLF -- because every other blob was
# already LF. That measurement is what makes renormalising safe here instead of a mass rewrite.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Listed AFTER the general rule because the last matching line wins, and the trust root keeps an
# unconditional `text`: its whole reason for being here is that the key must never be re-encoded by a
# content heuristic.
*.pub text eol=lf
*.pem text eol=lf
